  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis is conclusive: **Tryptophan metabolism** (KEGG map00380) is the dominant affected pathway (FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 625.5), with five of the eight input metabolites (L-tryptophan, serotonin, melatonin, 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid, and quinolinic acid) matching. A highly specific sub-pathway — **Tryptophan catabolism leading to NAD⁺ production** (Wikipathways WP4210; FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 1174.8) — is also strongly enriched, confirming that the kynurenine arm of tryptophan degradation is the primary perturbed branch.\n\n**Key driver metabolites and verified relationships:**\n- **L-Tryptophan (C00078)** is the entry-point substrate for both the serotonin/melatonin biosynthetic arm and the kynurenine degradation arm. KEGG graph analysis confirms L-tryptophan is bidirectionally reachable from serotonin (C00780) via a two-hop path through 5-hydroxytryptophan (C00643), consistent with the canonical serotonin biosynthesis route. It is also upstream of 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid (C00632) via a four-hop forward path through known kynurenine-pathway intermediates.\n- **Quinolinic acid (C03722)** occupies a terminal position in the kynurenine arm, serving as the direct precursor to niacin/NAD⁺. It is annotated in both Tryptophan metabolism (KEGG) and Nicotinate and nicotinamide metabolism (KEGG map00760), confirming its dual role.\n- **3-Hydroxyanthranilic acid (C00632)** is an established intermediate in the kynurenine pathway between tryptophan and quinolinic acid.\n- **Serotonin (C00780)** and **melatonin (C01598)** sit on the separate, faster tryptophan hydroxylation arm; their co-enrichment alongside kynurenine intermediates suggests treatment broadly redirects tryptophan flux.\n\n**Biological significance:** Coordinated changes across both the serotonin/melatonin axis and the kynurenine/quinolinic acid arm point to a global re-routing of tryptophan metabolism. This pattern is consistent with inflammatory or oxidative-stress conditions (e.g., IFN-γ activation of IDO1) that shunt tryptophan toward the kynurenine pathway, depleting serotonin and melatonin while generating neuroactive quinolinic acid. The presence of 3-methyladenine (an autophagy and AMPK activator) alongside O-phosphoethanolamine (a membrane phospholipid precursor) hints that the metabolic perturbation may also intersect with phosphoethanolamine metabolism and autophagic signaling, which can cross-talk with tryptophan pathway regulation. The convergence on tryptophan catabolism as the top hit provides a clear, evidence-based biological story centred on disrupted tryptophan utilisation.",
